WEBVTT

1a
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:10.620
This is Orion X, Phoenix Edition, version
three point zero: a live USB cyberdeck for
incident responders working in networks
they cannot trust. It boots from a USB

1b
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:21.457
stick on Debian 13 and runs with every
change held in memory. It needs no
network, makes no connection on its own,
and installs nothing on the machine you
are standing at.

2a
00:00:22.257 --> 00:00:26.699
The first boot asks for a name for the
deck and an account, with thirty-second

2b
00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:31.426
defaults. Pick the no-questions entry in
the boot menu and it asks nothing at all.

3a
00:00:32.226 --> 00:00:39.626
The desktop is XFCE, themed as a
cyberdeck. The panel shows the mesh you
are joined to, the events seen on the bus,

3b
00:00:39.626 --> 00:00:47.091
and the hosts on the local network. The
Cockpit is one click away, and every Orion
tool lives under one Orion menu.

4a
00:00:47.891 --> 00:00:57.048
The Cockpit's live tab is where you see
and hear the activity around you: a
scrolling event stream, a threat-pressure
gauge that decays over time, a network

4b
00:00:57.048 --> 00:01:06.323
sparkline, subsystem lights, the deck's
own vitals, and the current posture. This
feed is the built-in demo traffic; on a
real deck it is the live event bus.

5a
00:01:07.123 --> 00:01:14.056
Everything that detects something
publishes once, to one event bus. Rain,
the audible notifier, turns severity into

5b
00:01:14.056 --> 00:01:19.844
sound and speech. The Cockpit shows the
same line. What you hear and what you see
always agree.

6a
00:01:20.644 --> 00:01:29.819
The Awareness tab puts live health, the
firewall, the next hop and DNS on one
page, with sparklines for packets,
processor, memory and disk. Threat posture

6b
00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:38.461
has three tiers, from passive watching to
decoys and canaries, and the deck tells
you whether a tier is actually enforced
before it claims it is.

7a
00:01:39.261 --> 00:01:46.088
When an alert is your own scanner, squelch
it for a while or tune it out for good.
Every answer states whether the change

7b
00:01:46.088 --> 00:01:50.884
survives a reboot; on a stick without
persistence it does not, and the deck says
so.

8a
00:01:51.684 --> 00:01:58.911
Decks find each other on the local network
and form a WireGuard mesh with no hub.
Keys are made on the deck when you join,

8b
00:01:58.911 --> 00:02:05.071
and a team pre-shared key is one command
away. Team chat runs over that mesh on
your own Matrix server.

9a
00:02:05.871 --> 00:02:13.715
Nebula is the on-device assistant: a local
language model, verified against its hash
at every boot, listening on the deck only.

9b
00:02:13.715 --> 00:02:21.003
Its tools can look at packet captures and
artifacts, but they cannot improvise
commands, and nothing leaves the deck.

10a
00:02:21.803 --> 00:02:29.218
Underneath is a curated forensics toolkit:
Wireshark and Zeek for the wire,
Volatility for memory, capa and YARA for

10b
00:02:29.218 --> 00:02:34.779
binaries, and a synthetic training capture
to practise on, with no real malware in
it.

11a
00:02:41.579 --> 00:02:46.528
The Orion Workbench is the analyst's
toolbox in a browser: what is on the deck,

11b
00:02:46.528 --> 00:02:53.168
what you can add, and the open-source
intelligence tools, with God's Eye and
Pivotglass beside CyberChef.

12a
00:02:53.968 --> 00:02:58.778
Pivotglass is the adversary-infrastructure
hunting deck. It runs locally, keeps

12b
00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:04.989
hypotheses, evidence and contradictions as
separate records, and ships with an
offline learning case.

13a
00:03:05.789 --> 00:03:16.482
The full User Guide is on the image and on
GitHub. The ISO is larger than GitHub's
file limit, so it ships in signed parts:
download them, join them, verify the hash,

13b
00:03:16.482 --> 00:03:25.243
and write the image to a USB stick. It is
tested on one reference laptop and in
QEMU, so your bug reports are welcome.
That is Orion X.
